# Copy this file to .env and uncomment what you want to change.
# Environment variables that are already set take priority over this file. Settings marked (also in Settings)
# can instead be changed from any signed-in device; a value set here wins and locks that setting.

# Port and network interface to listen on. 127.0.0.1 = only through Tailscale (tailscale serve) or this computer.
# BEAM_PORT=8765
# BEAM_HOST=0.0.0.0

# Where Beam keeps everything: items, files, devices, sign-ins, settings, logs (default: ./data).
# BEAM_DATA=D:\Beam
# At every start Beam makes that folder private to the account it runs as (Windows: plus SYSTEM and
# Administrators; elsewhere chmod 700). keep = leave its permissions as they are (a share or service that needs them).
# BEAM_DATA_ACL=keep
# Where the built apps are handed out from (default: ./dist). Docker uses /data/dist.
# BEAM_DIST=D:\Beam\dist
# Refuse to start when the data folder has no key (instead of creating a new, empty Beam), e.g. a disk that
# isn't mounted yet. Set it once Beam is set up.
# BEAM_REQUIRE_DATA=1

# The address your devices use to reach Beam, for pairing links, QR codes and ntfy (also in Settings).
# Normally learned automatically from the first visit through Tailscale.
# BEAM_PUBLIC_URL=https://my-pc.tail1234.ts.net

# Only on the OLD machine after a move: send every device to the new address. Normally done with
# "node server.js moved-to <address>" or automatically by import-from.
# BEAM_MOVED_TO=https://beam.tail1234.ts.net

# Delete items after this many days (0 = keep until you delete them). Undelivered items get up to three times as
# long; pinned ones stay (also in Settings).
# BEAM_RETENTION_DAYS=14
# Keep at most this many items; the oldest delivered, unpinned ones go first (also in Settings).
# BEAM_MAX_ITEMS=500
# Largest upload allowed, in MB.
# BEAM_MAX_UPLOAD_MB=4096
# Cap on everything stored, in GB (the oldest delivered, unpinned items make room). 0 = only the disk limits it.
# BEAM_MAX_STORAGE_GB=0
# Upload chunk size in MB; lower it if a reverse proxy limits request sizes.
# BEAM_CHUNK_MB=8

# --- Backups (1.8.1) ---
# Beam saves a backup of itself (an export: the key, sign-ins, devices and their apps' settings, settings, items)
# every BEAM_BACKUP_HOURS (0 = never) into BEAM_BACKUP_DIR, and keeps the newest BEAM_BACKUP_KEEP. Default folder: a
# "backups" folder next to the data folder. Another drive or a NAS share keeps them safe from a failing disk too.
# The files people sent go in while they add up to at most BEAM_BACKUP_FILES_MB.
# Restore: stop Beam, then: node server.js import <backup> --force
# BEAM_BACKUP_DIR=D:\Beam-backups
# BEAM_BACKUP_HOURS=24
# BEAM_BACKUP_KEEP=14
# BEAM_BACKUP_FILES_MB=1024

# --- Signing in with Tailscale ---
# Devices signed in to these Tailscale accounts sign in to Beam by themselves (comma-separated). Beam also
# learns the accounts of devices you sign in, and the first account to use a brand-new Beam.
# BEAM_TAILSCALE_OWNERS=you@example.com
# Turn automatic sign-in with Tailscale off (also in Settings).
# BEAM_TAILSCALE_SIGNIN=0
# Talk to tailscaled through its LocalAPI socket instead of the tailscale command (Docker).
# BEAM_TAILSCALE_SOCKET=/var/run/tailscale/tailscaled.sock
# tailscaled's outbound HTTP proxy, for reaching other tailnet machines from a userspace-networking container.
# BEAM_TAILNET_PROXY=http://127.0.0.1:1055
# Don't ask Tailscale anything (no machine names, no Tailscale sign-in).
# BEAM_TAILSCALE=off

# --- Behind a reverse proxy other than tailscale serve ---
# Its addresses (IPs or CIDRs, comma-separated). Beam believes X-Forwarded-For/-Proto/-Host only from these and
# from this computer itself. Without it, every device looks like the proxy and nothing is linked automatically.
# BEAM_TRUSTED_PROXIES=172.17.0.1,192.168.1.10
# Maximum simultaneous connections.
# BEAM_MAX_CONNECTIONS=1000

# The master key, instead of data/key. Leave unset: a generated key is much stronger, and "sign out all
# other devices" can't change a key that is set here.
# BEAM_KEY=

# --- Optional: phone push notifications through ntfy (https://ntfy.sh) ---
# Use a long random topic name, since anyone who guesses it can read the notifications.
# BEAM_NTFY=https://ntfy.sh/beam-replace-with-something-random
# BEAM_NTFY_TOKEN=
# Put the text / file name in the notification (it then passes through the ntfy server).
# BEAM_NTFY_PREVIEW=0
# Don't notify about items sent *from* these devices (comma-separated device names).
# BEAM_NTFY_SKIP=iPhone

# ---- Beam Family (1.7): the family's chat, its own server (node family/server.js; docs/FAMILY.md) ----
# Where people open it (invite links, notifications). Beam's own server reads it too: its apps then show a
# "Beam Family" link.
# BEAM_FAMILY_URL=https://your-machine.your-tailnet.ts.net:8443
# BEAM_FAMILY_DATA=C:\Beam\family
# BEAM_FAMILY_PORT=8766
# BEAM_FAMILY_NAME=Family
# The Tailscale login that becomes the owner on its first visit (default: whoever owns this machine in Tailscale).
# BEAM_FAMILY_OWNER=you@example.com
# BEAM_FAMILY_MAX_UPLOAD_MB=2048
# BEAM_FAMILY_MAX_STORAGE_GB=100
# (1.9) Direct connections (fast links, big uploads): STUN servers to find the way (comma-separated; "local": the same
# network only; "off": none), and the UDP ports they use (a firewall rule can let the home network in on just these).
# BEAM_FAMILY_STUN=stun:stun.l.google.com:19302,stun:stun.cloudflare.com:3478
# BEAM_FAMILY_DIRECT_PORTS=41700-41799
# Beam Family's backups (1.8.1), as Beam's: the database (a snapshot taken while it runs), its keys and pictures, and the
# files while they add up to at most BEAM_FAMILY_BACKUP_FILES_MB. Default folder: the "backups" folder next to its
# data folder. Restore: node family/server.js stop, then: node family/server.js restore <backup> --force
# BEAM_FAMILY_BACKUP_DIR=D:\Beam-backups
# BEAM_FAMILY_BACKUP_HOURS=24
# BEAM_FAMILY_BACKUP_KEEP=14
# BEAM_FAMILY_BACKUP_FILES_MB=1024
